#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# longest_common_subsequence

#% text
# `longest_common_subsequence(a, b)` gives the longest run of consecutive elements that the lists (or strings) `a` and `b` both contain.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For strings the result is a string; for lists a list. The first such run in `a` is returned when several have the maximal length.

#% item
# `longest_common_sequence` allows gaps.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
longest_common_subsequence("abcdef", "xbcdy")
#% output
#= "bcd"

#% cell
longest_common_subsequence([1, 2, 3, 4], [0, 2, 3, 5])
#% output
#= [2, 3]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Nothing in common:

#% cell
longest_common_subsequence("abc", "xyz")
#% output
#= ""

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `longest_common_sequence`
